# A rate-dependent Species observable over complexes that merge and
# split (issue #79).
#
# Where dynamic_rate_species_obs_model pins the cost of the defect, this
# one pins the bookkeeping the fix leans on.  `Chains` is read by a rate
# law, so it is settled after every event; `ChainsCtl` is the same
# pattern left unread, so it keeps the ordinary once-per-sample cadence.
# The two share the per-molecule contribution tables and the pool's
# dead-complex side channel, and only one of them is flushed on the
# per-event path — so a flush that consumed a notification the other
# still needed shows up as the two disagreeing.
#
# A(s,t) polymerises into chains rather than capping at dimers, so a
# bond op moves molecules that are not themselves bond endpoints between
# complexes — the case per-complex tallies are most exposed to.
#
# The rate law drives P -> Q, a pool the polymerisation chemistry never
# touches, so the observable moves for reasons entirely outside the rule
# whose propensity reads it.
#
# Paired with: tests/cpp/dynamic_rate_species_obs_test.cpp

begin model
begin parameters
    A_tot   200
    P_tot   400
    kf      0.01
    kr      0.05
    kt      0.002
end parameters

begin molecule types
    A(s,t)
    P()
    Q()
end molecule types

begin seed species
    A(s,t)  A_tot
    P()     P_tot
    Q()     0
end seed species

begin observables
    Molecules  A_bound    A(s!+)
    Species    Chains     A(s!1).A(t!1)
    Species    ChainsCtl  A(s!1).A(t!1)
    Molecules  Q_tot      Q()
end observables

begin functions
    chain_rate() = kt*Chains
end functions

begin reaction rules
    A(s) + A(t) <-> A(s!1).A(t!1)  kf, kr
    P() -> Q()                     chain_rate()
end reaction rules
end model

begin actions
simulate({method=>"nf",t_end=>60,n_steps=>12,print_functions=>1})
end actions
